15:{"attempt_id": "P0013-A001", "depth": "first_pass", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The example has a symmetric law and exp(Z_n) in [n^(-1/2),n^(1/2)]. Its expectation is 1-1/sqrt(n)+(1+n^(-1))/(2), which tends to 3/2. It is not a random-graph construction.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Complement symmetry and uniform-integrability obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "Need a sharp upper bound and uniform integrability at the sqrt(n) scale; complement symmetry only yields the lower bound.", "problem_id": "P0013", "recorded_at": "2026-09-06T17:37:31Z", "result": "The theta product inequality theta(G) theta(complement G)>=n and complement symmetry of G(n,1/2) give E theta>=sqrt(n) by arithmetic-geometric mean. A proof of concentration on a logarithmic scale would still need tail control: set Z_n to plus or minus (log n)/2, each with probability 1/(2 sqrt(n)), and zero otherwise. Then Z_n tends to zero in probability and variance, but E exp(Z_n) tends to 3/2.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_limited_argument"}
16:{"attempt_id": "P0014-A001", "depth": "first_pass", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The exact identity is stronger than the general product inequality. The symmetric rare-tail example in P0013 has Z tending to zero in probability yet E cosh Z tending to 3/2, so concentration alone is insufficient.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Vertex-transitive complement identity", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "Must estimate the exponential tails of the logarithmic theta fluctuation for the dependent circulant adjacency structure.", "problem_id": "P0014", "recorded_at": "2026-09-06T17:37:31Z", "result": "A circulant graph is vertex transitive, so theta(G) theta(complement G)=n. Its random model is complement invariant. For Z=log(theta(G)/sqrt(n)), Z has the law of -Z and E theta/sqrt(n)=E cosh Z. The conjecture is therefore equivalent to E(cosh Z-1) tending to zero.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_limited_argument"}
18:{"attempt_id": "P0016-A001", "depth": "first_pass", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "Full spark holds almost surely. Adding rows increases the Gram matrix, so the minimum relevant frame bound is attained among M-row subsets. Both bounds use union/inclusion inequalities and never assume independence among overlapping minors.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Union small-ball lower bound and one-normal upper bound", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "The exponential lower bound and polynomial upper bound are far apart. Sharp extremes of the strongly dependent minors remain uncontrolled.", "problem_id": "P0016", "recorded_at": "2026-09-06T17:37:31Z", "result": "For a standard M-by-M real Gaussian matrix A, sigma_min(A)<=t implies that some column has distance at most sqrt(M)t from the span of the other columns: project a minimizing unit vector equation using a coordinate of magnitude at least 1/sqrt(M). Each such distance has the law |N(0,1)|, so P(sigma_min<=t)<=sqrt(2/pi) M^(3/2)t. There are at most 4^M M-row submatrices of a (2M-1)-by-M frame. Therefore its minimum omega exceeds exp(-(log 4+epsilon)M) with probability tending to one. Conversely fix M-1 rows and a unit normal u. The M remaining row projections are independent N(0,1), and omega is at most their minimum absolute value, giving omega<=C log(M)/M with probability tending to one for sufficiently large C.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_limited_argument"}
46:{"attempt_id": "P0046-A001", "depth": "first_pass", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The root-degree estimate is the finite sum of per-edge addition probabilities. Combined with the known opposite cost inequality, the proposed construction would prove equality, not merely a weak approximation statement.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Connection to the cost equality obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "No dependent sprinkling construction was found. This reduction explains why independent sprinkling or a generic connectivity argument does not settle the question.", "problem_id": "P0046", "recorded_at": "2026-09-06T17:47:59Z", "result": "If the free forest is already connected, taking its own configuration gives the requested extension with no added edges. Otherwise, an invariant connected extension with each edge added with probability at most epsilon would have expected root degree at most E deg_F(o)+deg_G(o) epsilon. The standard invariant-graphing bound would then imply cost(Gamma)<=E deg_F(o)/2=1+beta_1(Gamma) after epsilon tends to zero.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_limited_argument"}
152:{"attempt_id": "P0150-A001", "depth": "first_pass", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The probability that k sampled points all select the delta_0 component is(1-1/n)^k. The Gromov-Hausdorff distance from a compact space to a point is half its diameter. Each X_n still has full-support measure.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Minimal assumptions for heavy-tailed critical metric convergence structural reduction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "This is a precise failure of weak measured convergence to control thin appendages. A uniform lower bound on masses of fixed-radius balls is the missing type of estimate.", "problem_id": "P0150", "recorded_at": "2026-09-06T20:19:16Z", "result": "Let X_n=[0,1] with the ordinary metric and probability measure(1-1/n)delta_0+(1/n)Lebesgue. Every fixed finite iid sample from this measure lies at0 with probability tending to1, so its distance matrices converge to those of a point. Yet the spaces all have diameter1 and cannot converge in Gromov-Hausdorff distance to a point.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_limited_argument"}
334:{"attempt_id": "P0340-A001", "candidate_full_solution": true, "depth": "full_candidate_proof", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "Independent induction and telescoping give the same exact tree inequality. Exact checks passed for6918 ordered binary trees and23 rational height/occupation systems; five deliberately false identities were rejected. The initial float-contaminated checker failure is retained.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Exact tree identity, source-specific recurrence or explicit limiting obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "The source scale is contradicted and a linear expectation limit is derived, but there is no independent review or originality certificate. No LLN, variance, CLT or closed form for the series constant is claimed.", "problem_id": "P0340", "proof_path": "proofs/P0340_beta_tree_drawn_length_counterexample_candidate.md", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T02:27:06Z", "result": "For every full binary tree, drawn length equals the sum of all internal subtree heights plus the root height, and is at most the sum of all leaf depths. Hence its expectation is O(n*log^2 n), already refuting the proposed rate. A stronger proof gives E drawn length/n -> sum_(m>=2) a(m)*E height(T_m)/m, a finite constant at least2. A geometric halving bound proves E height(T_m)=O(log^2 m), and a uniform dyadic occupation bound controls the series tail.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_candidate"}
335:{"attempt_id": "P0341-A001", "candidate_full_solution": false, "depth": "individual_analytic_attempt", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "Both constructions use the exact root identity a(n,n)=1, nonnegativity and the established pointwise formula. No computed rate is needed. The stronger dyadic occupation bound in P0340 proves validity for suitable uniformly decreasing tails and explains why it does not cover these sparse root-sensitive tests.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Exact tree identity, source-specific recurrence or explicit limiting obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "The unrestricted ansatz is false. A comprehensive sharp two-parameter error theory and the best admissible class of tests remain unresolved here; therefore the broader numbered problem is only partly answered.", "problem_id": "P0341", "proof_path": "proofs/P0341_beta_tree_sparse_occupation_counterexample_candidate.md", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T02:27:06Z", "result": "The bounded test f(j)=1 when j is a power of2 and0 otherwise has finite limiting sum S because a(2^r)=O(r*2^-r). Along n=2^K, the certain root visit contributes1 in addition to every fixed initial set of terms tending to S. Thus the liminf is at least S+1, contradicting the proposed limit S. The test g(j)=j*f(j) separately refutes the divergent-series clause: the actual sum is at least2^K whereas the proposed truncated sum is of order K^2.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_partial"}
339:{"attempt_id": "P0345-A001", "candidate_full_solution": false, "depth": "individual_analytic_attempt", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The upper bound is proved in detail in P0340 using a geometric exponential moment, not by integrating a probability-only asymptotic. The lower bound uses that the maximum dominates every sampled leaf and that the leaf fluctuations are of smaller order(log n)^1.5.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Exact tree identity, source-specific recurrence or explicit limiting obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "The upper estimate is too loose to determine a common limit. Dependence among candidate deep branches and the tail behavior beyond a typical-leaf CLT remain the obstruction.", "problem_id": "P0345", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T02:27:06Z", "result": "A tagged chain halves its size with conditional probability at least1/(2H_(n-1)); coupling to a negative-binomial waiting time and taking a union bound over leaves gives E maximum height=O(log^2 n). The uniform random-leaf CLT gives the lower bound maximum height/(log n)^2 >=3/pi^2-o(1) in probability. Thus the order can be obtained without identifying the extremal constant.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_partial"}
342:{"attempt_id": "P0348-A001", "candidate_full_solution": false, "depth": "individual_analytic_attempt", "full_problem_solved": false, "independent_check": "The identity follows by cutting every edge at level h: the surviving portion above that level is a full binary tree with one more boundary branch than internal nodes, unless it is empty. It gives W(0)=n and sum_h W(h)=drawn length; balanced and comb trees verify both relations.", "independent_review": "none", "method": "Exact tree identity, source-specific recurrence or explicit limiting obstruction", "novelty": "not_claimed", "obstruction": "Only the limiting mean profile and its summability are obtained. Concentration, covariance, a random profile scaling limit and height regimes growing with n remain unproved.", "problem_id": "P0348", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T02:27:06Z", "result": "For every finite tree, W(h)=number of internal clades whose own graph height exceeds h, plus1 if the whole-tree height exceeds h. Thus E W_n(h)/n converges, for each fixed nonnegative integer h, to w(h)=sum_(m>=2) a(m)*P(height(T_m)>h)/m. The dyadic occupation bound in P0340 controls the tails uniformly, justifying the passage from fixed sizes to the full sum. Summing the limiting mean profile over h gives the linear expected-length constant from P0340.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "verification": "author_checked_partial"}
